UMLSVG = uml_svg(1230, 790, EDGES, legend_y=765)

HOW_TO_READ = ('<b>How to read a box.</b> Top: the class name (dashed border = interface; &laquo;enum&raquo; = a fixed '
 'list of values). Middle: its fields, the state it holds. Bottom: its methods. <b>The arrows.</b> Hollow triangle = '
 'implements. Filled diamond = owns: the service owns every trip, every driver and the index. Plain arrow = references: '
 'a trip points at its status, its two locations and the driver it is riding with. Dashed green = handed in through '
 '<code>configure()</code> or <code>setClock()</code>. Dotted blue = notifies. <b>Where state lives:</b> the driver has '
 'the two fields that move on their own &mdash; a volatile location written by the ping path and an '
 '<code>AtomicReference</code> status that is the one contended cell in the system; the trip has its own status plus the '
 'table of legal moves, and is the only class allowed to write it; the index has the grid and nothing else; the service '
 'has the maps, the handed-in rules and the order of operations, and one more cell worth naming: <code>activeTrip</code>, the rider-to-trip map that is claimed with <code>putIfAbsent</code> so a double tap cannot become two rides. The service holds no lock of its own. Notice what is <i>not</i> '
 'here: no Dispatcher class, because dispatch is four lines inside <code>requestRide</code>; no state classes, because '
 'a trip\'s states carry no behaviour; and no ETA field anywhere, because an ETA is a question, not a thing to keep up '
 'to date.')

# ============================================================ page 04: the code
CODE_INTRO = ('Read it with page 03 open in a second tab if you want the diagram beside it. The green comment above each '
 'class and method says what it does; read only those first for the shape, then the bodies for the mechanics. Each copy '
 'button copies that whole file for your IDE. Below Main.java: Extensions.java (every follow-up\'s reference code, with '
 'an <code>ExtDemo</code> main that runs all of it) and FailureTests.java (nine blocks of claims proven; '
 '<code>javac Main.java Extensions.java FailureTests.java &amp;&amp; java FailureTests</code> prints ALL PASS).')

# ============================================================ page 05: follow-ups and practice
IMPLEMENT_CARD = ('<div class="card"><div class="ch"><h3>0 &middot; Implement the system</h3>'
 '<button class="timer" data-min="60">start 60:00</button></div><div class="cb"><div class="prompt">' + PROMPT + '</div>'
 'Before typing, write your six to eight clarifying questions (fleet size and straight-line-versus-routed distance '
 'first); then type in the order of Main.java: the three enums, the Money helpers, the Clock, the Location record with '
 'haversine, Rider and Vehicle, then Driver with its AtomicReference and <code>reserve()</code>, then DriverIndex and the '
 'GridIndex, then the matching and pricing interfaces with one implementation each plus the surge wrapper, then the '
 'cancellation rule, the payment gateway and the observer, then Trip with its legal-edge table, then RideService with '
 'its two claims (the rider\'s slot, then the car) and its two orders (claim-then-write at the start, commit-then-charge at the end), then a main with the race.</div></div>')

FU = [
("Two riders tap for the same car in the same millisecond. Prove that only one of them gets it.", "non-functional", 10,
 "The race lives between reading a driver's status and writing it back. There is no lock: the status is an "
 "<code>AtomicReference</code> and the claim is <code>compareAndSet(AVAILABLE, RESERVED)</code>, so exactly one thread "
 "can get <code>true</code> for a given car, whatever the hardware does with the others. The proof is a count, not a "
 "log: fifty threads wait on one latch and all fifty request a ride against a fleet of one car, and the test asserts "
 "that exactly one trip exists. Then the same test runs with two hundred riders and forty cars and asserts forty trips "
 "and forty <i>distinct</i> drivers, which is the property that actually matters: no car was handed to two people, and "
 "no car was left idle while somebody was refused.",
 T("        // 1. the race", "        // 2. matching:")),
("The rider taps Book twice because the screen did not change fast enough. What does he get?", "functional", 6,
 "One ride, and a message naming it. The rider's live-ride slot is a second claim, made before the index is even "
 "asked: <code>activeTrip.putIfAbsent(riderId, \"(matching)\")</code> returns null for exactly one caller, and any "
 "tap that finds a value already sitting there is refused and told which trip is running. It is move 4 applied to "
 "the other end of the request &mdash; one contended cell, one atomic step &mdash; with "
 "<code>putIfAbsent</code> standing in for <code>compareAndSet</code> because the cell lives in a map rather than on "
 "an object. The slot goes back on every way out: at once if no car was found, so a failed request leaves nothing "
 "behind, and together with the car when the ride ends or is cancelled, <i>including</i> when the card declines, "
 "because money trouble must never lock a rider out of the app. The test fires twenty simultaneous taps from one "
 "rider at a fleet of five cars and asserts one ride and exactly one car out of the pool.",
 sect(src, "private final Map<String, String> activeTrip", "private final List<TripObserver> observers")
 + "\n" + T("        // 9. one rider", "        System.out.println(failures")),
("The airport is busy: charge 1.8x there between six and nine. Do it without opening the fare formula.", "twist", 8,
 "Surge is not a flag inside <code>NormalPricing</code>; it is a second pricing rule that holds the first one and "
 "multiplies whatever it charged. So a time-and-place surge is a <code>SurgeSource</code> &mdash; one method that "
 "returns basis points, reading the pickup and the timestamp it was handed &mdash; and the fare rule itself is not "
 "touched. The multiplier is integer basis points, not a double, so 1.8x is 18000 and the arithmetic stays exact. "
 "Because the wrapper is itself a <code>PricingStrategy</code>, a coupon wraps the surge and a toll wraps the coupon, "
 "and the order of the stack is visible at the call site instead of hidden in an if-chain. The zone version in "
 "Extensions.java computes the multiplier from the demand and supply the index already knows about.",
 sect(src, "class SurgePricing", "// \"do we charge for a cancellation") + "\n" + X("real surge", "pooling")),
("The rider was quoted 230.56 before he tapped, and surge doubled while he was in the car. What does he pay?", "twist", 8,
 "What he was quoted, applied to the ride he actually got. Two things make that true. The quote is the <i>same</i> "
 "pricing rule run on an estimated <code>FareBasis</code> &mdash; the straight line stretched by a road factor of "
 "1.35, and minutes at an assumed 22 km/h &mdash; so a quote and a bill can never disagree about how a fare is "
 "built, only about the distance and the time in it. And the multiplier is frozen at the instant of the quote and "
 "kept against the quote id, so the bill re-runs the base rule on the kilometres the car really drove and then "
 "multiplies by the basis points the rider agreed to. In Main.java that is one field and no new interface: the trip "
 "remembers its quote id, and <code>FareBasis</code> &mdash; the value object a rule is allowed to read &mdash; "
 "grows one more number. The reference code quotes 230.56 at 1.5x for an estimated 7.1 km and then bills the real "
 "8.0 km, 24-minute ride at 258.00, still 1.5x, however calm the zone has become since.",
 X("the upfront fare", "Runs every extension")),
("You used a compare-and-set rather than a lock. Does dispatch actually run in parallel, or have you fooled yourself?", "non-functional", 6,
 "It runs in parallel, and the code counts rather than claims. Nothing in the matching path takes a lock: the "
 "nine-cell read, the scan over the roughly nine hundred cars it returns, the choice and the trip object all run "
 "concurrently for every rider in the city. The only mutual exclusion is two atomic instructions of about twenty "
 "nanoseconds each, and each involves only the callers who actually collided. The service keeps a "
 "<code>lostRaces</code> counter for exactly this question: in the two-hundred-rider, forty-car test it reads 159 "
 "&mdash; a hundred and fifty-nine claims lost by a nanosecond &mdash; and the same run still produces exactly forty "
 "trips and forty distinct drivers, with nobody queued and nobody wrongly refused. Notice where the cost really is: "
 "sixty microseconds of scanning against forty nanoseconds of claiming, which is why the first upgrade is a finer "
 "index and not a cleverer lock. The one real lock is a trip's own monitor, wanted by that trip's rider and its "
 "driver, and <code>cancelTrip</code> holds it deliberately across the fee decision so a fee can never be priced from "
 "a state the cancellation does not then use.",
 sect(src, "Optional<Trip> requestRide", "/** The car is at the kerb")),
("The card declines after the ride is over. What is the state of the system?", "functional", 10,
 "Recorded, consistent, and retryable. A ride cannot be un-driven, so the order at the end is the opposite of the order "
 "at the start: price it (pure, and it may throw), record that the ride ended, put the car back in the pool, and only "
 "then call the gateway &mdash; outside every lock, with the trip id as the idempotency key. A decline leaves the trip "
 "at COMPLETED_UNPAID with its distance and fare already stored and the driver already AVAILABLE, because a card "
 "problem must never strand a driver. <code>retryPayment</code> finishes it later and the state machine refuses a "
 "second attempt on an already-paid trip. The failure test proves the money moved exactly once even when the retry is "
 "called twice, which is the gateway's key doing its job rather than the state machine's.",
 sect(src, "Trip endTrip(", "Trip cancelTrip(")),
("Two hundred thousand cars are online, four thousand of them are in one downtown cell, and the suburb has none.", "non-functional", 9,
 "The volume is already answered: location lives in a grid, so \"who is near here\" is nine bucket lookups whatever "
 "the fleet size, and a ping only touches the grid when the car crosses a cell boundary. The downtown cell is the "
 "known weakness of a uniform grid, density skew, and the fix is resolution "
 "rather than a different idea: smaller cells and a wider ring where the city is dense. Because <code>DriverIndex</code> "
 "is an interface, that is a new class and one line at the composition root; a production system uses a quadtree, a "
 "geohash prefix or S2 cells, which is the same trick with the resolution chosen per region. The suburb is the same "
 "problem upside down: nine cells that hold nobody. The same knob answers it &mdash; widen the ring until somebody "
 "appears, then stop at a cap. Each extra ring is (2r+1) squared bucket lookups, so widening is cheap and bounded, "
 "and the cap is a product decision rather than a technical one: a car twenty minutes away is a worse answer than "
 "\"no cars right now\", and sending it also strips the next suburb of its own car.",
 X("a denser city", "persistence")),
("The driver ignores the request for fifteen seconds. Now what?", "twist", 10,
 "The claim becomes a lease. Today <code>reserve()</code> holds a car forever; with an acceptance window the car is "
 "held while his phone rings, and if he declines or says nothing the hold is released and the ride is offered to the "
 "next-nearest car. The loop is the one already inside <code>requestRide</code> with two extra lines &mdash; a "
 "deadline, and a <code>release()</code> on the way out &mdash; and the driver who was just offered it is dropped from "
 "the candidate list so he is not asked twice. In the state machine it is one extra edge: ASSIGNED back to REQUESTED. "
 "A real system replaces the phone stand-in with a scheduled task that releases the lease when the deadline passes, "
 "which is the same code with a timer in front of it.",
 X("the driver declines", "fairness")),
("Ops says nearest-first starves the car at the edge of the hotspot, there is a river in the way, and the rider wants to know how many minutes.", "twist", 10,
 "Both are new matching rules, and neither touches anything else. For starvation: among the cars that are close "
 "enough, send the one who has been idle longest &mdash; distance stays the first filter, because fairness inside five "
 "kilometres is fair and fairness across a city is a rider waiting twenty minutes. For the river: a straight line is a "
 "lie, so the rule asks an injected <code>RouteProvider</code> for the ETA and sends the car that will <i>arrive</i> "
 "first, which is often not the nearest one. Each is a new class plus one changed line at the composition root; the "
 "service, the trip machine, the compare-and-set and the index are untouched, which is the whole point of having put "
 "the rule behind an interface in move 3. The third question uses the same seam and stores nothing: \"three minutes "
 "away\" is not a field anybody keeps up to date, it is a question answered when the screen asks it, from the "
 "driver's last ping and the trip's own status &mdash; the time to the kerb before the rider is in, the time to the "
 "destination once the meter is running. Keeping it as a field would mean fifteen thousand pings a second fanning out "
 "to every open app, and a number that is wrong between fan-outs.",
 X("fairness", "minutes, not kilometres") + "\n" + X("minutes, not kilometres", "coupons and tolls")),
("Cancellation. Who pays, and how do you know the fleet is not quietly draining?", "functional", 8,
 "The fee is a rule, not an if-chain: the policy is handed the state the trip is being cancelled in and how long the "
 "rider has waited, and returns paise &mdash; free while nobody has been sent, free for two more minutes because the "
 "driver has only just set off, thirty rupees once he is really coming. The order is the usual one: "
 "compute the fee, run the guarded transition (which throws, changing nothing, if the meter is already running), then "
 "free the car, then charge the fee outside any lock. One subtlety is worth saying before you are asked: reading the "
 "state and cancelling in it are one step, inside the trip's own monitor. Split them and a rider who cancels in the "
 "same instant the driver reaches the kerb is priced as ASSIGNED, free, on a trip that has already become ARRIVED "
 "&mdash; a kerbside cancellation given away by a two-line race. Draining is the other failure nobody tests for: "
 "every path that takes "
 "a car out of the pool &mdash; completion, cancellation, a declined card &mdash; has a matching <code>release()</code> "
 "in the same method, and the failure tests assert the driver is AVAILABLE again after each one.",
 sect(src, "class StandardCancellation", "/** Thrown when the gateway") + "\n" + sect(src, "Trip cancelTrip(", "/** One trip by id")),
("UberPool: one car, three riders, picked up and dropped along the way.", "twist", 8,
 "Not a new state machine. A pooled ride is a trip with several riders and one fare to divide, so two things change "
 "and nothing else. Matching scores the <i>detour</i> a new rider would add to the live route instead of the distance "
 "to the pickup, which is another <code>MatchingStrategy</code>. The fare is divided in proportion to the kilometres "
 "each rider was actually in the car, and it has the exact problem a Splitwise split has: the shares must add up to "
 "the fare to the paise, so the last leg absorbs the rounding. The trip's status, the claim, the cancellation rule and "
 "the payment path are all unchanged, which is why this is an eight-minute answer rather than a redesign.",
 X("pooling", "ratings")),
("Ratings, analytics and receipts all want to hear about a completed trip. And one of them throws.", "twist", 5,
 "They are observers, and they hear about it <i>after</i> the state has already moved. The rating book listens for a "
 "trip reaching COMPLETED, remembers that this trip is unrated, and accepts exactly one star rating for it later; the "
 "service never learns that ratings exist, and a rating average can later feed a matching rule without anybody "
 "touching the trip. The throwing listener is the part worth proving: <code>publish</code> calls each observer inside "
 "a try/catch, so a push gateway that is down cannot stop a ride from completing or a driver from being freed. The "
 "failure test registers a listener that throws on every single call and then checks the trip completed with the right "
 "fare and the car went back into the pool.",
 X("ratings", "a denser city") + "\n" + T("        // 7. a listener that throws", "        // 8. the ping path:")),
("Persist it. And now there are two servers.", "twist", 8,
 "The trip becomes rows behind a repository and the service's code does not change, only what it was handed. The "
 "interesting half is the claim: a JVM's <code>AtomicReference</code> cannot arbitrate between two processes, so the "
 "driver's status becomes a column and the claim becomes <code>UPDATE driver SET status='RESERVED' WHERE id=? AND "
 "status='AVAILABLE'</code> &mdash; one row updated means you won, zero rows means somebody else did. That conditional "
 "UPDATE <i>is</i> compare-and-set, performed by the database for the whole fleet. The trip insert carries ON CONFLICT "
 "DO NOTHING on the trip id so a retried request creates one trip, and the guarded transition becomes an UPDATE with "
 "the old status in the WHERE clause so two settlement workers cannot both complete the same ride. The rider's slot "
 "goes the same way and gets easier: a unique partial index on (rider_id) where the trip is still open, so the second "
 "tap fails on the insert instead of on a map.",
 X("persistence", "the upfront fare")),
("Where does time come from, and how do you test a twenty-four-minute ride?", "design", 3,
 "The service has a <code>Clock</code> it was handed, and nothing else in the system reads the wall clock: the trip's "
 "timestamps, the duration the fare is built from and the wait a cancellation fee depends on all come through it. A "
 "test hands in a clock backed by a single variable, starts a ride, moves the variable forward by twenty-four minutes "
 "and ends it, and can then assert the fare to the paise rather than asserting that it is roughly right. The same seam "
 "makes the cancellation grace period and the acceptance lease testable, because both are deadlines measured against "
 "an injected now rather than a found one.",
 "/** Where time comes from. Injected, so a test can decide that a ride took exactly twenty-four minutes. */\n"
 "interface Clock { long nowMs(); }\n\n"
 "// on the service: handed in, defaulted, never read from the wall clock inside a method\n"
 "private Clock clock = System::currentTimeMillis;\n"
 "void setClock(Clock c) { clock = c; }\n\n"
 "// in a test: pick the instant, then move it\n"
 "long[] now = { 1_700_000_000_000L };\n"
 "svc.setClock(() -> now[0]);\n\n" + T("        // 5. the fare comes from", "        // 6. the card declines AFTER")),
("Which pattern is where, which SOLID letter is where, and where would a Factory or the State pattern earn its place?", "design", 8,
 "Answer this backwards, because that is how the page was built: name the move, then the pattern it produced. "
 "Strategy came out of move 3, Decorator out of the same move's wrapper, Observer out of move 4, State-as-a-table out "
 "of move 6, Facade out of move 2 &mdash; the code lines are in the table there. The half interviewers actually "
 "listen for is the half about patterns you did <i>not</i> use. Singleton earned nothing: the service is handed to "
 "its callers, which is exactly why a test can build a fresh one with a declining card. Factory is \"not yet\", and "
 "this is the enum-versus-subclass question in disguise: a vehicle tier carries no behaviour of its own, only three "
 "numbers, so it is an enum and the tier-to-rate <code>EnumMap</code> is already the registry a factory would be. "
 "Give a tier behaviour &mdash; XL needing a seat check, PREMIER a driver-rating floor &mdash; or let tiers arrive as "
 "strings from configuration, and the enum becomes a class and the map becomes a factory, on that day and not "
 "before. Builder never earns it here: a trip has six required fields and no optional ones, which is a constructor.",
 "// Strategy: three rules behind three one-method interfaces, handed in, never built by the service\n"
 "interface MatchingStrategy   { Optional<Driver> select(Location pickup, VehicleType type, List<Driver> candidates); }\n"
 "interface PricingStrategy    { long price(FareBasis basis); }\n"
 "interface CancellationPolicy { long feePaise(TripStatus cancelledIn, long waitedMs); }\n"
 "void configure(MatchingStrategy m, PricingStrategy p, CancellationPolicy c, PaymentProcessor pay) { ... }\n\n"
 "// Decorator: a pricing rule that HOLDS a pricing rule, so wrappers stack in the order the call site declares\n"
 "PricingStrategy fare = new SurchargePricing(new CouponPricing(new SurgePricing(new NormalPricing(), zone), 2000, cap), toll);\n\n"
 "// Observer: the service announces; it does not know what a phone or a rating is\n"
 "private void publish(Trip t, TripStatus from, TripStatus to) {\n"
 "    for (TripObserver o : observers) try { o.onTrip(t, from, to); } catch (RuntimeException e) { /* log */ }\n"
 "}\n\n"
 "// State, as a TABLE: the whole lifecycle in six lines, checked before any field is written\n"
 "m.put(TripStatus.IN_PROGRESS, EnumSet.of(TripStatus.COMPLETED_UNPAID));   // and never CANCELLED\n"
 "if (!LEGAL.get(status).contains(to)) throw new IllegalStateException(id + \": \" + status + \" -> \" + to);\n\n"
 "// Factory: not yet. The registry is already here; it earns the name when tiers come from config strings\n"
 "Map<String, long[]> byName = Map.of(\"GO\", new long[]{4000, 1200, 150}, \"XL\", new long[]{8000, 2000, 250});\n\n"
 "// Builder: never, here. Six required fields is a constructor; a trip has no optional ones\n"
 "new Trip(id, rider, driver, pickup, drop, type, clock.nowMs());\n"),
]

